---
// The context and options for one decision, ported verbatim from the standalone
// page this route replaces.
//
// Same split as OperatorDetail: `cutoverProgress.ts` holds what the page
// reasons about a decision — which steps it blocks, what we recommend and why —
// while the situation and the trade-offs stay as authored prose here, because
// nothing computes over them and a data structure would only make them harder
// to revise.
interface Props {
  id: string;
}
const { id } = Astro.props;
---

{
  id === 'D1' ? (
    <Fragment>
      <p class="q">
The domain is registered at GoDaddy and its DNS is managed there too — which is
              good news, because it means no slow registrar transfer is needed. But we've never
              had access, and this has been an open question since the 2026-07-02 investigation.
              Nothing can start without it.
      </p>
      <ul class="opts">
<li><b>Someone on the team has it</b> — point us at them and we'll walk them through it screen by screen; they make the edits, we never hold the credentials.</li>
              <li><b>Give us access directly</b> — GoDaddy supports delegate access, which can be revoked afterwards and doesn't mean handing over the account password.</li>
              <li><b>Nobody knows</b> — then finding out is genuinely the first task, and it may take longer than the rest of this page combined. GoDaddy's account recovery runs on the registrant email on file.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
  ) : null
}
{
  id === 'D2' ? (
    <Fragment>
      <p class="q">
Right now the site is behind a passphrase, so placeholders are fine — they read
              as work in progress. At cutover they stop being drafts and become what the
              organisation says about itself. The status page's "content to fill or remove"
              list is the checklist; the open items are three example sponsors rendering as
              real partners, five board members with no bios, a volunteer project still in
              draft, and a Give button that opens an email because there's no donation platform yet.
      </p>
      <ul class="opts">
<li><b>Close the list first</b> — fill or remove each item, then cut over. Slower, but nothing embarrassing goes live.</li>
              <li><b>Cut over and fix live</b> — the site is editable through the CMS and redeploys in a minute or two, so gaps can be closed after. Faster, but the gaps are public while they're open.</li>
              <li><b>Split it</b> — treat the sponsors specifically as blocking (they name organisations that aren't actually partners) and let the rest be fixed live.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
  ) : null
}
{
  id === 'D3' ? (
    <Fragment>
      <p class="q">
There are thirteen homepage design mockups (about 20MB) published at an
              unguessable URL for the board to look at. They're hidden from search engines but
              have no passphrase — the unguessable address <em>is</em> the access mechanism,
              and it's a link that's actively been shared. The moment the site becomes public,
              that folder ships to <code>harvardintech.com</code> along with everything else.
              Anyone who has the link — or is forwarded it — keeps working access to
              abandoned design directions on the organisation's real domain.
      </p>
      <ul class="opts">
<li><b>Leave it</b> — it's already effectively public and nobody is going to guess the URL. Costs nothing, changes nothing, and the board's link keeps working.</li>
              <li><b>Move it to the review site</b> — it stays reachable for the board behind the same passphrase they already use, and never lands on the public domain. Their current link breaks and has to be re-sent.</li>
              <li><b>Remove it</b> — the mockups have served their purpose and the chosen direction is built. Their link breaks permanently.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
  ) : null
}
{
  id === 'D4' ? (
    <Fragment>
      <p class="q">
<b>Half of this is now settled.</b> This runbook is no longer an ordinary file
              inside the site — it is a route excluded from the public build, so it cannot
              appear on <code>harvardintech.com</code> at all. What is left of D4 is the status
              page, which is still a raw file that would
              ship to the public domain at cutover. Their passphrase is a deterrent
              that runs in the browser — the text is technically in the downloaded page — so on
              a public domain it protects against casual readers and not much else. What's
              behind it is internal candour: what isn't built, what we still need from you,
              and where the domain's records live.
      </p>
      <ul class="opts">
<li><b>Move the status page too</b> — the gated track exists precisely for this, and it is what the runbook already does. Your link changes once, and it can never leak onto the public domain by accident.</li>
              <li><b>Leave it</b> — the passphrase plus an unlisted URL is what's protecting it today, and that stays true after cutover. Simplest, and no links change.</li>
              <li><b>Remove it at cutover</b> — it's a launch-phase artifact. Cleanest, but you lose the shared status view exactly when the project moves into its next phase.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
  ) : null
}
{
  id === 'D5' ? (
    <Fragment>
      <p class="q">
The switch itself is minutes and can be scheduled tightly, but the day matters
              for a reason that isn't technical: if something is wrong, we want people
              available to notice and decide, not to find out on Saturday night.
      </p>
      <ul class="opts">
<li><b>A weekday morning</b> — the whole team is around, everyone can check email straight after, and there's a full working day to react. This is what we'd pick.</li>
              <li><b>Right before an event or campaign</b> — worst case; traffic peaks exactly while the change is settling.</li>
              <li><b>After a specific milestone</b> — e.g. once the board has signed off, or the content list is closed. Tell us the milestone and we'll work back from it.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
  ) : null
}
